# Lichess Predictor

Train a model that estimates the expected score of the side to move from a Lichess game position. A prediction of `1.0` means the side to move eventually won, `0.0` means they eventually lost, and draws are trained as `0.5`.

## Feature Set

The pipeline turns each sampled ply into two feature groups:

- Board tensor: 16 planes, oriented from the side-to-move perspective. It includes own pieces, opponent pieces, and castling rights.
- Game context vector: ratings for side/opponent, rating difference, time control, clock fractions, side-to-move engine eval from PGN comments when present, ply, material balance, rated flag, title flags, speed bucket, and ECO bucket.

The extractor intentionally excludes final-only leakage such as `Termination` and rating diff. Those are useful for analysis but should not be available when predicting an in-game position.

## Model Architecture

The production model in `HybridWinModel` is a hybrid neural net:

- A compact CNN encodes the 8x8 board tensor.
- An MLP encodes tabular context such as ratings, clock, time control, and eval.
- A fused head outputs one logit trained with binary cross entropy on expected score labels.

This is a good first architecture because it keeps the board spatial, lets non-board context matter, and can stream minibatches without preprocessing the full 2 TB corpus. For very large training runs, the next upgrade would be residual blocks plus embeddings for high-cardinality metadata such as opening, player pool, month, and time-control family.

When PyTorch is unavailable, the CLI falls back to a NumPy online logistic model so the parser and labels can still be smoke-tested.

## Install

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/python -m pip install -e .
```

For the neural model:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m pip install -e '.[torch]'
```

## Run On A Sample

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m lichess_predictor.cli inspect tests/fixtures/sample.pgn --max-examples 3

.venv/bin/python -m lichess_predictor.cli train tests/fixtures/sample.pgn \
  --backend auto \
  --max-games 1 \
  --sample-every-n-plies 1 \
  --batch-size 8 \
  --epochs 3
```

The same commands work on compressed Lichess shards:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m lichess_predictor.cli train data/raw/lichess_db_standard_rated_2024-01.pgn.zst \
  --backend torch \
  --max-games 100000 \
  --sample-every-n-plies 6 \
  --batch-size 1024 \
  --output data/checkpoints/standard-2024-01.pt
```

## Scaling Notes

- Stream `.pgn.zst` directly with `zstandard`; never decompress full monthly shards to disk.
- Cap `--max-games` and `--max-positions` for experiments, then increase shard coverage gradually.
- Use `--sample-every-n-plies` to reduce correlation and control the positions-per-game multiplier.
- Train by month or by a shuffled manifest of monthly files. For multi-worker training, assign whole shards to workers to avoid seeking inside zstd streams.
- Keep validation temporally separated, for example train on older months and validate on a newer month.
- Filter bots by default. Consider separate models for bullet/blitz/rapid/classical because clock pressure changes outcome probabilities.
